Z||000000||84 2in1||06-19-2001||05:30 PM||||Anyone have any advice on porting the head out on a 2.5?Thanx -B-||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||06-19-2001||05:40 PM||||Yeah, don't make the ports bigger just smooth out the sharp edges. Don't make the runners smooth either or you will end up with fuel puddling.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||06-19-2001||08:33 PM||||Maybe more than you want to know:<P>Responses to my porting questions: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007799.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007799.html</A> <P>Before and after photos: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html</A> <P>The whole sordid story: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009419.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009419.html</A> <P>Good luck!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Brucepts||06-19-2001||09:05 PM||||One of the best improvements you could do is a three angle seat and valve cut on the intake. This will almost double your low lift flow up to .150" A wider seat will also enhance low lift flow. After you have the seat on the face of the valve, backcut the angle almost to the seat mark. On the front side of the valve have the valve margin angle cut to break the sharp edge. This will allow the flow to form a "cone" quicker on the port side of the valve. The duke motor is not a high rpm motor so anything you can do to help low lift flow will make a difference. Like was stated before you want to keep the port size the same and just cleanup the casting flash. This motor needs port velocity not high rpm flow numbers. With velocity comes high flow at low rpm's. Hopefully in the very near future I can get some serious time on my flowbench with a 2.5 head.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000004||Coupe'N||06-19-2001||10:18 PM||||I've got an 87 2.5 with 111,000 miles. I was told if the topend of the engine is better than the bottom (heads/intake Versus block/pistons), it could ruin the bottom end. Eat it up even. Is this true. How do I go about finding out if the bottom end of the engine is good enough to do a good topend job?<BR>Thanks for any info...<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||5
Z||000005||Coupe'N||06-21-2001||12:04 AM||||Hello Hello Hello ???  Bumpin to the top~!<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||06-21-2001||05:27 PM||||If you have good oil pressure and the motor is in good running order than your bottom end won't blow apart if you modify the top end a little. If you try to rev your engine beyond what it was designed to do or stuff gobs of nitrous into it than no motor new or old will last very long. <P>The guy who told you that is just one of those no it all story tellers who makes things up and gives bogus information. Do what Brucepts said, it's excellent information and he is definitely right.  <P>||||reg||1
